d with her old one. If Olson, played by Elisabeth Moss, bolts the firm, it will probably be to head her own agency -- and possibly get married. That is, if she's still interested in such an old-fashioned tradition. In recent seasons, Don's ex-wife, Betty (January Jones), has lost herself amid all the turmoil. She sees herself through the eyes of her spouses, and though husband Henry Francis (Christopher Stanley) has been far more supportive than Don, he's a busy man. And Sally, Don's daughter (Kiernan Shipka), is proving to be a handful. On the one hand, she's obviously bright; on the other, she's a teenager and starting to rebel. In recent seasons she's run away and started sneaking cigarettes, and she's always fighting with her mother. You could see her hitchhiking to Woodstock, or at least dropping out of school. Anything's possible, but given all that the character has been through -- divorces, affairs, office politics, morose late-night rides with Glen Bishop -- it's a bit on the nose, isn't it? Instead, try this: It's April 1, 1970. Richard Nixon is signing legislation banning cigarette ads on radio and television, reminding Don of the day 10 years earlier when he came up with the Lucky Strike campaign that began the series. No fool, he had seen this day coming years before. He'll fix himself a drink, ponder buying an avocado-colored refrigerator, clean out his ashtray and leave the show the way he arrived: on top of the zeitgeist, unable to accept his past and utterly, inscrutably alone.\\n\", \"answer\": \"The final season of the popular TV series \\\"Mad Men\\\" premieres on April 1st, marking the end of an era. Set in the 1960s, the show follows the lives of employees at a fictional advertising agency in New York City. The last episode ended with the moon landing in July 1969, and fans are eagerly anticipating what will happen next. The show's creator, Matthew Weiner, has remained tight-lipped about the finale, but based on previous seasons, it is likely that the show will continue to explore themes of disintegration and change. Characters such as Don Draper, the creative director played by Jon Hamm, and Peggy Olson, an up-and-coming advertising executive played by Elisabeth Moss, are expected to play a significant role in the final season. The show is known for its accurate depiction of the cultural and social changes of the 1960s, including the rise of feminism, the civil rights movement, and the drug culture. As the show comes to a close, fans are speculating about the fate of the characters and the future of the advertising industry.\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n"}
