a. Valencia faces 15 charges, including three counts of aggravated assault, three counts of armed robbery and possession of a deadly weapon by a prohibited possessor. Cohen-Metzger said he had a prior record. His next court appearance is May 18. He is in the Pima County Jail. Marana police on Thursday told CNN's Miguel Marquez that a store gun lock was still on the rifle when it was recovered. The wire that goes through the trigger and the lever to reload the gun were loose enough to allow it to still be used, police said. CNN's Tony Marco, Jason Hanna and Dominique Debucquoy-Dodley contributed to this report.\\n\", \"answer\": \"An Arizona police officer, Michael Rapiejko, who previously faced an excessive force lawsuit in New York has been involved in another high-profile incident. In February, he drove his patrol car into a man who had fired a rifle into the air minutes earlier and had pointed a gun at another officer. No charges were brought against the officer for the incident. Valencia, the suspect, survived and was hospitalized before being criminally charged. Rapiejko's actions in Marana have stirred debate about what type of force police should use. The Marana police chief defended the officer's actions, stating that \\\"deadly force was authorized\\\". This is particularly because the suspect had a firearm and posed a threat to public safety. This case raises questions about what actions are considered justifiable use of force by law enforcement officials.\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n", "add_special": true}
