 the new base as part of a plan to reduce the U.S. military footprint in Okinawa, but Okinawans remain unconvinced and find that the democratic expression of their opposition is resolutely ignored. Shared values?\\n\", \"answer\": \"Japanese Prime Minister, Shinzo Abe, delivered a historic speech to the US Congress yesterday but disappointed many with his remarks on Japan's controversial wartime past. His revisionist views on history have upset many nations, particularly China and South Korea, and his latest comments have been seen as ambiguous and evasive. Before the speech, pressure was put on Abe by Japan to use the language of the 1995 Murayama Statement, the most forthright apology and acknowledgement of its wartime responsibility. Instead, Abe said &ldquo;History is harsh. What is done cannot be undone&rdquo;, and expressed &lsquo;eternal condolences&rsquo; about the loss of American lives in World War II. He also controversially referred to Japan's 'comfort women' system (which involved many sexual slaves) in terms of human trafficking, in an attempt to shift responsibility from the Japanese military and government.\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n","generated":""}
