mps of filler on the surface, additional sanding with coarser sandpaper (such as 120-grit) may be necessary.\\n\\nPassage 2 provides an alternative method for filling pitted metal. First, secure an 80-grit flapper wheel to a grinder and sand the pitted metal until no rust or old paint remains on the surface. Then, remove the flapper wheel and attach a wire wheel to the grinder's arbor. Use the wire wheel to remove any debris from the pitted areas. Finally, spray degreasing cleaner on the surface of the metal and repeat the sanding process until no rust or old paint remains.\\n\\nPassage 3 provides step-by-step instructions for repairing pitted pot metal or zinc die cast parts. The process involves cleaning the pitted area with a wire brush, applying a filler material, and then sanding and painting the repaired area.\\n\\nTherefore, based on the provided passages, the best way to fill pitted metal is to first sand the area aggressively with a sanding block and 80-grit sandpaper, followed by additional sanding with coarser sandpaper if necessary. Alternatively, you can use a grinder with an 80-grit flapper wheel to remove rust and old paint, followed by a wire wheel to remove debris from the pitted areas.\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n","generated":""}
