onto Mercury's surface at 8,750 mph on April 30. The probe was launched in 2004 and began its orbit of Mercury on March 18, 2011. However, MESSENGER is now running out of fuel and NASA has no way to save the spacecraft. Engineers at Johns Hopkins University have been using the remaining fuel on board to keep the spacecraft in orbit and will make a final manoeuvre on Friday, April 24. Despite the loss, scientists are celebrating the mission's success. The spacecraft sent back data indicating that ice in Mercury's shadowed polar regions would be more than two miles thick if spread over an area the size of Washington, and took some stunning photographs of the planet.\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n", "add_special": true}
