ffaloes, however, is still permissible. However, beef traders in the country strongly reacted to the decision and called a month-long strike, which ended Wednesday. The traders refused to even slaughter buffaloes and deprive the state of all bovine meat. They have now vowed to file a case in the state's high court. Red meat lovers weren't too delighted either, arguing the government doesn't have a right to interfere in an individual's personal preference. Maharashtra is not the only Indian state to tighten its laws on cow slaughter. Haryana state has implemented a maximum punishment of 10 years in prison, the toughest penalty in the country. Rajnath Singh, India's Home Minister has promised that he would do all to devise a country-wide law against cow slaughter.\\n\", \"answer\": \"In Malegaon, India, police require identity cards for cattle after a state-wide ban on beef sales and consumption. Owners must provide photos and details of their cows, and the database aims to help solve cow slaughter cases. Over 100 owners have complied, but beef traders have responded with a month-long strike and plan to challenge the decision in court. The move is part of a broader effort to tighten cow slaughter laws in India, with Haryana implementing the toughest penalties and the home minister promising a country-wide law.\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n", "add_special": true}
